<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-chapter-4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Functions of Several Variables</title>

<introduction>
  <p>
    In this chapter, we shift our focus from geometric objects like curves and surfaces to functions that
    depend on more than one independent variable. Functions of several variables arise naturally when
    modeling real-world phenomena, such as temperature, pressure, or elevation, where a quantity
    varies across a region rather than along a single path.
  </p>

  <p>
    We begin by learning how to visualize functions of two variables using graphs, level curves, and
    vertical traces. These tools provide multiple perspectives on the same function and help make
    sense of higher-dimensional information. We then extend the ideas of limits and continuity to
    multivariable functions, building directly on concepts from single-variable calculus.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chapter continues by introducing partial derivatives, differentials, and differentiability.
    These tools describe how a function changes with respect to each variable independently and how
    those changes combine locally. By the end of the chapter, you should be comfortable analyzing
    the local behavior of multivariable functions, preparing you for optimization and gradient-based
    methods in the next chapter.
  </p>
</introduction>


